    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-48763",
      "knowledge_point_id": "660e7de2-aeea-55ad-971b-3c137ccb1521",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining a Rate of Change Given a Related Rate and an Explicit Function",
      "problem": "Given that $y=3x-1$ for $x=x(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2$ at the point where $x=-1,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3x-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $x=-1,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot (3)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
