    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-342707",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0f2818fc-787e-5b46-8a7a-c5fbea7905a6",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining an Angular Rate Given a Related Rate",
      "problem": "Given that $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot [-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})] \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
