      "problem": "Given that $r=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
