      "math_academy_id": "q-115772",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0f2818fc-787e-5b46-8a7a-c5fbea7905a6",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining an Angular Rate Given a Related Rate",
      "problem": "Given that $r=\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
