    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-342740",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0b47a8b7-722d-564e-8e7a-ad07577d6f6b",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Rate of Change of an XY-Coordinate",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=3\\,\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x+1}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3+1}+2(3)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+6) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-25\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
