      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{x}^{3}+16x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{x}^{3}+16x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{3}+16x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (12{x}^{2}+16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(-\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (12\\cdot (3)^{2}+16)\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s} \\\\  & =-31\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
