      "problem": "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-3{e}^{x}$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}-3{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{x}^{2}-3{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}-3{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4x-3{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(4\\cdot (2)-3{e}^{2})\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s} \\\\  & =2(8-3{e}^{2})\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
