      "knowledge_point": "Determining a Rate of Change Given a Related Rate and an Explicit Function",
      "problem": "Given that $y=x^3+e^x$ for $x=x(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2$ at the point where $x=1,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3}+{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2$ and $x=1,$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3\\cdot {1}^{2}+{e}^{1}) \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3+e) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{3+e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
