      "knowledge_point": "Determining an Angular Rate Given a Related Rate",
      "problem": "Given that $r=\\sin\\theta$ for $\\theta = \\theta(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ at the point where $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10902",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0b47a8b7-722d-564e-8e7a-ad07577d6f6b",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Rate of Change of an XY-Coordinate",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=x+e^x$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=5\\,\\textrm{cm}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1+{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}$ and $x=5$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(1+{e}^{5})\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
